Filter Expression Gotchas

1. Filters do NOT short-circuit

In a single run like `[<var>is[blank]then[yes]else{!!field}enlist-input<var>]`, ALL operators execute sequentially. `then[yes]` does NOT stop the chain — `enlist-input` still runs and can wipe out the result.

```
<!-- BROKEN — enlist-input runs even after then[yes] -->
[<var>is[blank]then[yes]else{!!field}enlist-input<var>match<x>]

<!-- CORRECT — use ~ (else) between separate runs -->
[<var>is[blank]then[yes]] ~[{!!field}enlist-input[]match<var>then[yes]]
```

The `~` operator means "use next run only if previous returned empty".

---

2. `-[operator...]` does NOT subtract from the accumulated result

`-[<var>]` and `-[[literal]]` subtract those values — fine. But when the run starts with an OPERATOR, its source is all tiddlers, not the accumulated result. `-[prefix[min:]]` subtracts every wiki title starting with `min:` (usually none) — it does not remove matching accumulated items.

```
<!-- BROKEN — subtracts nothing -->
[[min:3]] [[x]] -[prefix[min:]]

<!-- CORRECT — :filter tests each accumulated item -->
[[min:3]] [[x]] :filter[!prefix[min:]]
```

Negated operators (`!has[...]`, `!prefix[...]`, `!is[...]`) work fine inside `:filter`.

---

3. `get[field]` returns nothing (not "") for missing/empty fields

`get[fieldname]` on a missing or empty field returns **no output** — not an empty string. So `is[blank]` after `get` on a missing field has no input and produces nothing.

```
<!-- BROKEN — get returns nothing, is[blank] has no input -->
:filter[<currentTiddler>get[my-field]is[blank]]

<!-- CORRECT — use !has for missing/empty fields -->
[<tiddlers>!has[my-field]]
```

`has[field]` returns true only when the field exists AND is non-empty.

---

4. Bare `[a/b/c]` is NOT a title — it parses as operator `a` with regexp `/b/c/`

A single-bracket run containing slashes is read as `[operator/regexp/]`. A subtraction like `-[a/b/c]` silently does nothing. The warning `Filter <op> has a deprecated regexp operand` naming an operator you never wrote is the giveaway.

```
<!-- BROKEN -->
[all[tiddlers]] -[a/b/c]

<!-- CORRECT — double brackets for a title literal, or a variable -->
[all[tiddlers]] -[[a/b/c]]
```

---

5. `\function` body is single-line — unless you close it with `\end`

Everything after `\function name(params)` on the SAME line is the filter. If the rest of the line is blank, the body runs until a `\end` line; without `\end`, the function is empty and every later pragma leaks into the tiddler body as text.

```
<!-- BROKEN — no \end: empty function, line 2 becomes body text -->
\function myFunc()
  [all[tiddlers]tag[X]]

<!-- CORRECT — entire filter on one line -->
\function myFunc() [all[tiddlers]tag[X]] :filter[get[status]match[active]]

<!-- CORRECT — multi-line with \end -->
\function myFunc()
[all[tiddlers]tag[X]] :filter[get[status]match[active]]
\end
```

Pragmas (`\function`, `\procedure`, `\import`, …) only register at the TOP of a tiddler, before any content.

---

6. Operator chaining after `function[]` — watch the brackets

After `function[name]`, the `]]` looks like a closing run. Chaining operators AFTER `]]` puts them outside the run.

```
<!-- BROKEN — "Filter error: Missing [" -->
[function[myFunc]]count[]

<!-- CORRECT — chain inside the same run -->
[function[myFunc]count[]]

<!-- CORRECT — new run with + prefix -->
[function[myFunc]] +[count[]]
```

---

7. Dates: `<now>` writes LOCAL time, `format:date` reads stored dates as UTC

- `<<now "DDth MMM YYYY">>` — display
- `[<now "YYYY0MM0DD">]` — works inside filters; capture into a variable with `$let` for comparisons
- To STORE a date in a field, use the `[UTC]` prefix. Without it the digits are local time, and every later `format:date` display is shifted by the timezone offset.
- `format:date` tokens: `DDD` = long day name ("Monday", locale-dependent), `ddd` = short name, `dddd` = weekday number Mon=1..Sun=7. `MM` is NOT zero-padded — use `0MM`.

```
<!-- Filter comparison -->
<$let today={{{ [<now "YYYY0MM0DD">] }}}>
  [<today>compare:date:gt[20260101]]
</$let>

<!-- Storing a timestamp -->
<$action-setfield due={{{ [<now "[UTC]YYYY0MM0DD0hh0mm0ss0XXX">] }}}/>
```

---

8. `[[` and `]]` cannot be used as literal strings in filters

Brackets are filter syntax. You cannot produce them via `addprefix`/`addsuffix` operands — not even inside backtick templates. Store them in variables or fields and reference those.

```
<!-- BROKEN — [[ and ]] are parsed as filter delimiters -->
[<title>addprefix[[[]addsuffix[]]]]

<!-- CORRECT — brackets in variables -->
<$let ob="[[" cb="]]"
  wrapped={{{ [<title>addprefix<ob>addsuffix<cb>] }}}>
</$let>
```

To turn a list of titles into a re-enlistable string, use `+[format:titlelist[]join[ ]]` — it adds `[[…]]` only where needed (see tw-gotchas/titles).

---

9. `subfilter` with literal filter operands — nested brackets break parsing

`[subfilter[tag[sensitive]]]` does NOT work — the operand ends at the first `]`.

```
<!-- BROKEN -->
[subfilter[tag[sensitive]]]

<!-- CORRECT — {tiddler-reference} -->
[subfilter{$:/config/my-protection-filter}]

<!-- CORRECT — <variable> -->
<$let myFilter="[tag[sensitive]]">
  [subfilter<myFilter>]
</$let>
```

This applies to any operator that takes a filter as its operand (`subfilter`, `filter`, `reduce`, `map`, …). Same trap for any operand that contains `]`, e.g. a regexp with `[0-9]`: put it in a tiddler field and use `regexp{tiddler!!field}`.

---

10. JSON: chained `jsonget`/`jsonindexes` don't compose — use the multi-argument form

When iterating a JSON array with `:map`, chaining `jsonget<currentTiddler>jsonget[id]` silently returns empty. Pass the whole path to ONE operator instead:

```
<!-- BROKEN — returns empty for every item -->
[<cache>get[text]jsonindexes[]] :map[<cache>get[text]jsonget<currentTiddler>jsonget[id]]

<!-- CORRECT — multi-argument jsonget -->
[<cache>get[text]jsonindexes[]] :map[<cache>get[text]jsonget<currentTiddler>,[id]]

<!-- Nested arrays: jsonindexes also takes a path -->
[<tid>get[data]jsonindexes<idx>,[items]]
```

Deleting an array element with `jsonset[<idx>]` (no value) turns the array into an object with `"0"`, `"2"` keys — rebuild the array from the remaining elements instead. When writing JSON into a field, use compact JSON (no newlines) — a newline in a non-text field makes TW save the tiddler as `.json` instead of `.tid`.

---

11. `compare:integer` treats non-numbers as EQUAL to everything

`parseInt("abc")` is `NaN`, and TW's comparison falls through to "equal". So `[[abc]compare:integer:gteq[0]]` and `[[abc]compare:integer:eq[5]]` both KEEP "abc". Never use `compare:integer` to validate numeric input — use `regexp` with a digits pattern stored in a field.

---

12. `removeprefix`/`removesuffix` DROP non-matching titles

`[[a.md]] [[b.txt]] +[removesuffix[.md]]` returns only `a` — `b.txt` is dropped, not passed through. Select first, then strip: `:filter[suffix[.md]] :map[removesuffix[.md]]`.

---

13. Config tiddler text keeps the file's trailing newline

A `.tid` config body like `|` has text `"|\n"`. Using it as an operand (`split`, `match`, `removeprefix`, …) silently fails. Chain `trim[]` when reading config text: `[[$:/config/x]get[text]trim[]]`.
